  "question": {
    "text": "A solid sphere of uniform density and radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> applies a gravitational force of attraction equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on a particle placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>, distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>R</mi></math> from the centre O of the sphere. A spherical cavity of the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> is now made in the sphere as shown in the figure.<br />\rThe sphere with the cavity now applies a gravitational force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on the same particle placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>.<br />\rThe ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></math>&nbsp;will be<br />\r<br />\r&nbsp;&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/EM0016177_Question_1.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 125px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
